===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

<!--Compare [[w:ploidy#Haploid_and_monoploid]]-->
# {{senseid|en|genetics, broad sense, defined by typical gamete karyotype for the organism}} {{lb|en|genetics|broadly}} Having the number of [[chromosome]]s that a typical [[gamete]] has (in the given organism), namely, half the number that a typical [[somatic]] cell has (in the given organism).
#: {{hypo|en|monoploid<qq:1 set>|diploid<qq:2 sets>}}
#: {{cot|en|euploid|;|aneuploid|nonaneuploid|;|q4=more than 2|polyploid<qq:as follows>|triploid<qq:3>|tetraploid<qq:4>|pentaploid<qq:5>|hexaploid<qq:6>|heptaploid<qq:7>}}
## {{senseid|en|genetics, strict sense, defined by typical gamete karyotype for most organisms}} {{lb|en|genetics|strictly}} Having a single set of unpaired [[chromosome]]s {{ng|(which is the number that a typical [[gamete]] has in many organisms although not all)}}.
##: {{syn|en|monoploid}}
##: {{hypo|en|gynohaploid|allohaploid|polyhaploid}}
##: {{cot|en|euploid|;|aneuploid|nonaneuploid|;|diploid<qq:2>|;|q5=more than 2|polyploid<qq:as follows>|triploid<qq:3>|tetraploid<qq:4>|pentaploid<qq:5>|hexaploid<qq:6>|heptaploid<qq:7>}}
##: {{ux|en|Examples of '''haploid''' cells are [[prokaryote]]s and the [[gamete]]s of [[eukaryote]]s.}}

